'''boart''' (bōrt), ''n.'' Same as ''bort.''

'''boast<sup>1</sup>''' (bōst), ''v.'' [< ME. ''bosten'', ''boosten'', < ''bost'', boast: origin unknown. The W. ''bostio'', ''bostian'' = Corn. ''bostye'' = Gael. ''bosd'', boast, are from the E.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1†.''' To threaten; utter a threat.—'''2.''' To brag; vaunt; speak vaingloriously or exaggeratedly, as of one’s own worth, property, deeds, etc.
{{smaller block|<poem>''Booste'' not myche, it is but waast;
Bi boostynge, men mowe foolis knowe.</poem>
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 52.}}
By grace are ye saved through faith; . . . not of works, lest any man should ''boast.'' {{right|Eph. ii. 8, 9.}}}}
'''3.''' To glory or exult on account (of); speak with laudable pride.
{{smaller block|I ''boast'' of you to them of Macedonia. {{right|2 Cor. ix. 2.}}}}
'''4.''' To be possessed, as of something remarkable or admirable: often used jocosely.
{{smaller block|It [the cathedral] does not appear so rich as the smallest church, but ''boasts'' of a little organ, which sent forth singularly inharmonious cries.
{{right|''Darwin'', Voyage of Beagle, I. 4.}}}}
='''Syn.''' {{smaller|To bluster (about), vapor, crow (about a thing, or over a person), swell, talk big, put on airs.}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To brag of; speak of with pride, vanity, or exultation: as, to ''boast'' what arms can do.
{{smaller block|<poem>But let him ''boast''
His knowledge of good lost, and evil got.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xi. 86.}}
<poem>He ''boasts'' his life as purer than thine own.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Balin and Balan.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
'''2.''' To glory or exult in possessing; have as a source of pride: often in a jocose sense: as, the village ''boasts'' a public pump.
{{smaller block|<poem>God be thanked, the meanest of His creatures
''Boasts'' two soul-sides, one to face the world with,
One to show a woman when he loves her.</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', One Word More.}}}}
'''3.''' To magnify or exalt; make over-confident; vaunt: with a reflexive pronoun.
{{smaller block|They that trust in their wealth, and ''boast themselves'' in the multitude of their riches.
{{right|Ps. xlix. 6.}}
''Boast'' not ''thyself'' of to-morrow.
{{right|Prov. xxvii. 1.}}
Many there be that ''boast themselves'' that they have faith.
{{right|''Latimer'', 4th Serm. bef. Edw. VI. (1549).}}}}

'''boast<sup>1</sup>''' (bōst), ''n.'' [< ME. ''boost'', ''bost'': see the verb. The W. ''bost'' (= Corn. ''bost'' = Ir. and Gael. ''bosd''), a boast, is from the E.] '''1†.''' Clamor; outcry.
{{smaller block|He crakkede ''bost'' and swor it was nat so.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 81.}}}}
'''2†.''' Threatening; menace.—'''3.''' Brag; vaunting; language expressive of ostentation, pride, or vanity.
{{smaller block|<poem>Reason and morals? and where live they most,
In Christian comfort or in Stoic ''boast?''</poem>
{{right|''Byrom'', Enthusiasm.}}}}
'''4.''' A cause of boasting; occasion of pride, vanity, or laudable exultation: as, Shakspere, the ''boast'' of English literature.
{{smaller block|His Candle is alwayes a longer sitter vp then himselfe, and the ''boast'' of his Window at Midnight.
{{right|''Bp. Earle'', Micro-cosmographie, A Pretender to Learning.}}}}
='''Syn.''' Vaunt, brag. See ''boasting''<sup>1</sup>.

